  learn: { title: 'Visibility Modifiers: public vs private', subtitle: 'A property or method with no modifier is public — accessible from anywhere. Marking it private restricts it to code inside its own class only.', exampleTag: 'EXAMPLE', exampleTitle: 'A private property used internally', language: 'Kotlin', codeSnippet: ['class BankAccount(private val pin: Int, val balance: Int) {', '  fun checkPin(entered: Int) = entered == this.pin', '}', 'fun main() {', '  val acc = BankAccount(1234, 500)', '  println(acc.balance)', '  println(acc.checkPin(1234))', '}'], explanation: 'balance has no modifier, so it’s public: main() reads acc.balance freely. pin is marked private, so it can only be used by code INSIDE BankAccount, like checkPin — real Kotlin would refuse to compile acc.pin written from outside the class entirely.', keyIdeas: [{ number: 1, title: 'No modifier means public', description: 'Public members are accessible from anywhere that can see the instance.' }, { number: 2, title: 'private restricts access to the same class', description: 'Only code written inside that class can read or call a private member.' }, { number: 3, title: 'private keeps a class’s public interface focused', description: 'Internal details that other code shouldn’t depend on can be hidden from it entirely.' }], keyTakeaway: 'Mark a property or method private when it’s an internal detail; leave it public only when other code genuinely needs to use it directly.' },
